Ouch, ok, so my theory of it being related to the time is not true either :(. Since you're calling `render_by_line`, could you try to see via debug output what the values are for the `line: usize` parameter? When toggling the switch, you should get the function invoked also with line values that start at the top of the screen (so zero and onwards).
@tronical I've added debug output in `process_line`, and `line: usize` was start from 70 and end at 82 when toggling the switch. So it seems that only color changed area have been re-rendered.
The problem here is in the Slint implementation of the partial rendering for the software renderer.
The change in the Window background color is not making any area dirty.

As a workaround, you can use a top level Rectangle with a background.
@ogoffart Thank you for your solution : ). I think there are two problem now. One is dirty marking that you mentioned, and the other one is animate problem. As shown in video clip, animation not work as expected either. When configured animate, color dosen't animate but only show a darker or lighter color instead.
I think this is the same problem: the background color is not being tracked, so even if it animates, it doesn't mark anything dirty and so nothing gets repainted. Only other part of the UI that changes get repainted.
@ogoffart 
>I think this is the same problem: the background color is not being tracked, so even if it animates, it doesn't mark anything dirty and so nothing gets repainted. Only other part of the UI that changes get repainted.

You are right, i've tested your workaround, and animation works well too.
The skia renderer with partial rendering is affected, too.
I started working on this, but it's a little trickier, so postponed. But the basic idea I had is this:

1. Teach `WindowItem::render` to look like this:
```rust
  let mut rect = Rectangle::default();
  rect.background.set(self.background());
  backend.draw_rectangle(core::pin::pin!(rect).as_ref(), self_rc, size);
```
2. Teach the FemtoVG and Skia renderers to pick up a solid background color from the window item, if a rendering notifier is set, and use that color as clear color. This is to maintain compatibility. If this code path is taken, then the `draw_rectangle` implementation for those two renderers needs to be taught to *skip* doing its work if `self_rc` is the `WindowItem` which we picked the clear color off of.
3. In the future, change behaviour to require `Window { background: transparent; }` if applications want to render their own underlay in rendering notifiers, and that way eliminate step 2.
>     1. Teach `WindowItem::render` to look like this:
> 
> ```rs
>   let mut rect = Rectangle::default();
>   rect.background.set(self.background());
>   backend.draw_rectangle(core::pin::pin!(rect).as_ref(), self_rc, size);
> ```

Why not implement `impl RenderBorderRectangle for WindowItem`
end then 
```rs
backend.draw_border_rectangle(self, self_rc, size, &self.cached_rendering_data);
```

Seems simple enough.


The SoftwareRenderer also need to be changed so that it doesn't draw the background twice.
Yet, when using a transparent background, the question is whether to clear buffer or not before drawing. Right now, the software renderer fills the buffer with the background color.
But we don't do that, and draw the background as a rectangle, we will blend it over. Which might not be correct if the background is transparent.

Hence the special_case.

Maybe we could have a ItemRenderer::draw_background as a special case.

----

Another idea:
WindowItem::render could be like so:
```
// Record a dependency on the background without actually drawing it.
// This will force the tracker to be dirty.
let _ = self.background();
```

This will fix the issue. But will not simplify the code so that the swrenderer draw gradient background.

